      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(1,0).$",
